## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design privacy-first OTCA paths that enforce data residency, tenant isolation, and masking, going beyond basic streaming.

## Key Concepts

- OTCA data contracts, regional immutable storage, global masked aggregates
- Per-tenant masking (tokenization, redaction) and tenant-key management
- Data lineage, access controls, and residency verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskRecord(record, tenantKey) {
  const masked = { ...record };
  if (masked.email) masked.email = hash(masked.email + tenantKey);
  if (masked.phone) masked.phone = redact(masked.phone);
  // redact other PII fields as needed
  return masked;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test masking correctness and residency under synthetic bursts?
- How do you handle schema evolution for masked fields across regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-cloud, multi-tenant OTCA pipelines with governance and schema contracts.

## Key Concepts

- Federated schema registry with per-tenant Protobuf contracts
- Forward/backward compatibility and versioning
- Tenant isolation via topic scoping and ACLs
- Cross-cloud replication and data deletion/retention governance
- Observability and testing strategies

## Code Example

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";
package otca;
message TelemetryEvent {
  string tenant_id = 1;
  string service_id = 2;
  string event_type = 3;
  int64 timestamp = 4;
  int32 latency_ms = 5;
  string model_version = 6;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test per-tenant deletion across all stores?
- What are trade-offs of Pulsar vs Kafka in a multi-cloud OTCA setup?

## Why This Is Asked
This assesses practical thinking on per-tenant privacy, simple policy evaluation, and a lean streaming path. It tests ability to reason about data models, minimal latency, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policies
- Ingest-time policy evaluation
- Event schema with tenant partitioning
- Data privacy and auditability
- Simple test strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight policy evaluation skeleton
function evaluatePolicy(event, policyStore) {
  // policyStore: { tenant_id: { redact: [fields] } }
  const p = policyStore[event.tenant_id] || { redact: [] };
  const redacted = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(event.payload || {}));
  p.redact.forEach(f => { if (redacted.hasOwnProperty(f)) redacted[f] = "***"; });
  return { ...event, payload: redacted };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support dynamic policy updates without downtime?
- What metrics would you monitor to ensure privacy and latency goals?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world concerns: consent governance, dynamic schema evolution, cross-region data lineage, residency, and auditable privacy controls.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant consent governance and field-level filtering
- Edge filtering and region-local routing
- Schema evolution via Avro + schema registry with versioning
- Tamper-evident, immutable audit logs for lineage
- DSAR testing, data portability, and deletion guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
function filterFieldsByConsent(event, consentMap) {
  const allowed = consentMap[event.tenant_id] || [];
  const filtered = {};
  for (const k of Object.keys(event)) {
    if (allowed.includes(k)) filtered[k] = event[k];
  }
  return filtered;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle consent updates mid-stream without data loss?
- What metrics validate data minimization and retention policy?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical privacy controls in a real OTCA path, focusing on consent-driven data handling and per-tenant residency.

## Key Concepts
- Ingest pipelines with edge gateways and Kafka
- Privacy controls: redact/drop audio data, store digest only
- Per-tenant residency and retention windows

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(event) {
  if (!event.consent_opt_in) delete event.audio_payload;
  event.audio_digest = hash(event.audio_payload || '');
  delete event.audio_payload;
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test quota enforcement across tenants?
- How would you monitor privacy policy violations in near real time?
